{
  "format": "hsvg/2.0",
  "kind": "capability",
  "id": "remote-seats",
  "about": {
    "title": "Remote seats",
    "description": "A 2.1 draft, outside the 2.0 registry (D11, 10.7): seats on different devices, run by a host-run authority.",
    "version": "2.1.0-draft"
  },
  "core": ">=2.1 <3",
  "adds": [
    "seats on different devices: a host-run authority holds the experience's store and runs every input through the same checked path as a local input (A4.1)",
    "the wire verbs reuse the agent door (A4.1): join, view, legal, act and leave; a client sends {sessionId, seat, requestId, verb, key?}; sessionId and requestId are 1..64 portable characters, seat is a seat id, and key (only for act) is a key of the seat's legal list; the exports below give each verb's request (with) and reply (returns) and are the authority's wire verbs, not calls that content makes",
    "the request carries no identity: the authority uses the identity the host bound to the connection; it carries no expected revision: legal-list keys are single-use and tied to view, occupant and turn, and a used or stale key is refused with E-CHANGE-STALE",
    "the authority ignores a requestId it has seen in the last 256 from that seat; it replies {requestId, ok, revision, error?} and broadcasts {revision, public, view} per seat; join returns the latest snapshot, so a client that reconnects joins again",
    "no content grammar of its own: the same seats, turns and legal lists work locally or remotely",
    "privileged capability (10.7): the authority is host code and its network port is the host's own; the host turns remote seats on in its profile, and content changes nothing and needs no new grant (the fillers grant is still needed only for filler kinds beyond human)"
  ],
